+      "content": "*Don* and *doña* go before a first name: *don Manuel*, *doña Rosa*. They show respect for an older or well-regarded person and sound warmer than *señor* and *señora*, which go with a surname or stand alone. Never use *don* with a surname."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "title": "Recognising vos (Argentina and Uruguay)",
+      "content": "In Argentina and Uruguay, people say *vos* where other countries say *tú*. You do not need to use it. You need to recognise it when you hear it or meet it in a film or a book.\n\nIn the present, *vos* takes the infinitive, drops the final *-r* and stresses the last syllable: *hablar → vos hablás*, *comer → vos comés*, *vivir → vos vivís*. Stem changes disappear: *tú quieres* but *vos querés*, *tú puedes* but *vos podés*. Two common verbs are different: *tú tienes → vos tenés*, *tú eres → vos sos*. The command also drops the *-r*: *vení* (come), *mirá* (look), *decime* (tell me). *Te* and *tu* stay the same."
